---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import BmiCalculator from '@components/islands/BmiCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';

const calc = getCalculator('health', 'bmi-calculator')!;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How is BMI calculated?',
    answer:
      'Body Mass Index is your weight in kilograms divided by the square of your height in metres (BMI = kg ÷ m²). In imperial units the formula is 703 × pounds ÷ inches². This calculator handles the conversion for you in both systems.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a healthy BMI range?',
    answer:
      'The World Health Organization classifies a BMI of 18.5–24.9 as a healthy weight, below 18.5 as underweight, 25–29.9 as overweight and 30 or above as obese. This calculator also shows the weight range that would put you in the healthy category for your height.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is BMI accurate for everyone?',
    answer:
      'BMI is a useful population-level screening tool but it does not measure body fat directly. It can overestimate risk for muscular athletes and underestimate it for older adults with less muscle. Treat it as a starting point and consult a healthcare professional for a full assessment.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does BMI work for children?',
    answer:
      'This calculator is intended for adults aged 20 and over. Children and teenagers should use age- and sex-specific BMI-for-age percentiles from a pediatric growth chart instead.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What does BMI stand for?',
    answer:
      'BMI stands for Body Mass Index. It was devised in the 1830s by Belgian mathematician Adolphe Quetelet, so it is sometimes called the Quetelet Index. It is a ratio of weight to height, not a direct measure of body fat.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How can I lower my BMI?',
    answer:
      'Because BMI is weight over height squared, lowering it means reducing weight (height is fixed in adults). A modest, sustained calorie deficit is the usual route — estimate your needs with the calorie calculator — though muscular people should read BMI alongside body-fat percentage.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Calculate your Body Mass Index in metric or imperial units, see your WHO weight category on a color-coded scale, and find the healthy weight range for your height."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-17' }}
>
  <BmiCalculator />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Choose <strong>Metric</strong> (centimetres and kilograms) or <strong>Imperial</strong> (feet, inches and pounds).</li>
      <li>Enter your <strong>height</strong> and <strong>weight</strong> in the selected units.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate BMI</strong> to see your BMI, your WHO category and the healthy weight range for your height.</li>
      <li>After the first result, the figure and the marker on the colored scale update automatically as you adjust your numbers.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What Body Mass Index is</h2>
    <p>
      Body Mass Index is a single number that relates your weight to your height, used to
      screen adults for underweight, healthy weight, overweight and obesity. It needs only two
      measurements and no equipment, which is why clinicians and public-health bodies reach for
      it as a first-pass indicator. It estimates whether your weight is in a healthy proportion
      to your height — not how that weight is made up.
    </p>

    <h2>The BMI formula</h2>
    <p>In metric units, BMI is your weight in kilograms divided by the square of your height in metres:</p>
    <p><strong>BMI = weight(kg) / height(m)<sup>2</sup></strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>weight</strong> — your body weight in kilograms</li>
      <li><strong>height</strong> — your height in metres (175 cm = 1.75 m)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Working in imperial units, the equivalent is <strong>703 × weight(lb) / height(in)<sup>2</sup></strong>.
      The calculator converts your entries for you, so both systems return the same BMI to
      within rounding.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Take someone <strong>175 cm</strong> tall weighing <strong>70 kg</strong>. Their height in
      metres is 1.75, and 1.75<sup>2</sup> = 3.0625, so BMI = 70 ÷ 3.0625 ≈ <strong>22.9</strong>.
      That sits in the 18.5–24.9 band, a <strong>healthy weight</strong>. Entered in imperial
      units (about 5 ft 9 in and 154 lb) the same person comes out at 22.9 as well — the
      calculator converts between the systems for you.
    </p>

    <h2>Interpreting your result</h2>
    <p>The World Health Organization sorts adult BMI into four bands:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>BMI</th><th>Category</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Below 18.5</td><td>Underweight</td></tr>
        <tr><td>18.5 – 24.9</td><td>Healthy weight</td></tr>
        <tr><td>25.0 – 29.9</td><td>Overweight</td></tr>
        <tr><td>30.0 and above</td><td>Obesity</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The colored scale marks where you fall between these bands, and the result also shows the
      <strong>healthy weight range</strong> for your height — the weights that correspond to a
      BMI of 18.5 to 24.9. For someone 175 cm tall that range is roughly
      <strong>56.7 kg to 76.3 kg</strong>.
    </p>

    <h2>Where BMI falls short</h2>
    <p>
      BMI counts all weight the same, so it cannot tell muscle from fat or say where fat is
      stored. A muscular athlete may register as "overweight" with very little body fat, while
      an older adult who has lost muscle may sit in the "healthy" band yet carry elevated risk.
      Waist circumference, body-fat percentage and blood markers add the detail BMI leaves out.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Adults only.</strong> These cut-offs apply to people aged 20 and over; children and teens use age- and sex-specific BMI-for-age percentiles.</li>
      <li><strong>No body-composition detail.</strong> Two people with the same BMI can differ greatly in muscle and fat.</li>
      <li><strong>A screen, not a diagnosis.</strong> BMI flags who may benefit from a closer look, not a verdict on any individual's health.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related calculators</h2>
    <p>
      To see how much of your weight is fat rather than muscle, use the
      <a href="/health/body-fat-calculator">body fat calculator</a>; to find a weight target
      from height and sex, try the <a href="/health/ideal-weight-calculator">ideal weight
      calculator</a>. This is educational information, not medical advice. Speak with a qualified
      healthcare provider about your individual health.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
